# Models

## Three kinds of models

| Kind | What | Where | Built with |
|---|---|---|---|
| API schemas | request and response bodies | `api/<feature>.py` | Pydantic, subclass `ApiModel` (`api/schemas.py`) |
| Domain models | entities and value objects, with their rules | `domain/<feature>/` | frozen `@dataclass(frozen=True, slots=True)` |
{%- if with_database %}
| Persistence models | table rows | `infrastructure/db/models.py` | SQLAlchemy, subclass `Base` |
{%- endif %}

Pydantic is for boundaries, where untrusted data comes in or goes out: API schemas,
payloads of other services and of the LLM (parsed in the adapter){% if with_cache %},
cached values (in the cache adapter, see [caching.md](caching.md)){% endif %} and settings.
Inside the service the domain uses plain frozen dataclasses: no validation framework, no
serialisation concerns, and `domain` stays free of framework imports.

Each kind changes for its own reason (an API version, a business rule{% if with_database %}, a column{% endif %}),
so they are separate classes even when the fields look alike. Domain objects never leave
through HTTP directly, and API schemas never reach the application layer.

## Domain models

- Entities and value objects are `@dataclass(frozen=True, slots=True)`. Change one by
  building a new one (`dataclasses.replace`).
- Invariants are checked in `__post_init__` and raise a domain error (see
  [errors.md](errors.md)), so no entry point (HTTP, a job, a test) can build an invalid one.
- Ids: `type RecipeId = UUID` names the role; use `NewType` when mixing two kinds of id up
  must be a type error.
- Ports (Protocols) for a feature sit next to its models in `domain/<feature>/ports.py`.

The example used across these docs:

```python
# src/{{ package_name }}/domain/recipes/recipe.py
from dataclasses import dataclass
from uuid import UUID

from {{ package_name }}.domain.errors import ConflictError, NotFoundError, ValidationError

type RecipeId = UUID

MAX_SERVINGS = 50


class RecipeNotFoundError(NotFoundError):
    def __init__(self, recipe_id: RecipeId) -> None:
        super().__init__(f"Recipe {recipe_id} does not exist")


class RecipeAlreadyExistsError(ConflictError):
    def __init__(self, recipe_id: RecipeId) -> None:
        super().__init__(f"Recipe {recipe_id} exists already")


class InvalidServingsError(ValidationError):
    def __init__(self, servings: int) -> None:
        super().__init__(f"A recipe serves 1 to {MAX_SERVINGS} people, not {servings}")


@dataclass(frozen=True, slots=True)
class Recipe:
    id: RecipeId
    title: str
    servings: int

    def __post_init__(self) -> None:
        if not 1 <= self.servings <= MAX_SERVINGS:
            raise InvalidServingsError(self.servings)
```

```python
# src/{{ package_name }}/domain/recipes/ports.py
from typing import Protocol

from {{ package_name }}.domain.recipes.recipe import Recipe, RecipeId


class RecipeReader(Protocol):
    async def get(self, recipe_id: RecipeId) -> Recipe:
        """Raises `RecipeNotFoundError` when there is no such recipe."""
        ...


class RecipeWriter(Protocol):
    async def add(self, recipe: Recipe) -> None:
        """Raises `RecipeAlreadyExistsError` when the id is taken."""
        ...
```

## API schemas

- Subclass `ApiModel`: `extra="forbid"` rejects unknown fields instead of dropping them, and
  `frozen=True` makes instances immutable.
- Separate request and response models (`CreateRecipeRequest`, `RecipeResponse`), even when
  they share fields: they change for different reasons.
- Constraints go in the type: `Annotated[int, Field(ge=1, le=50)]`. Constraints used by
  several schemas become a named alias in `api/schemas.py`.
- Optional fields are explicit: `note: str | None = None`.
- Mapping lives on the schema: `to_domain()` on a request, `from_domain()` on a response.
  The route calls them; nothing else maps between API and domain.

A shared constraint:

```python
# add to src/{{ package_name }}/api/schemas.py
from typing import Annotated

from pydantic import StringConstraints

# A one-line text field: surrounding spaces removed, then 1 to 200 characters.
type ShortText = Annotated[
    str, StringConstraints(strip_whitespace=True, min_length=1, max_length=200)
]
```

The schemas of a feature live next to its router (routers: see
[architecture.md](../architecture.md#how-dependencies-reach-routes){% if with_database %}; the
recipes router is in [persistence.md](persistence.md#example-a-recipes-table){% endif %}):

```python
# src/{{ package_name }}/api/recipes.py
from typing import Annotated, Self
from uuid import UUID

from pydantic import Field

from {{ package_name }}.api.schemas import ApiModel, ShortText
from {{ package_name }}.domain.recipes.recipe import MAX_SERVINGS, Recipe, RecipeId


class CreateRecipeRequest(ApiModel):
    title: ShortText
    servings: Annotated[int, Field(ge=1, le=MAX_SERVINGS)]

    def to_domain(self, recipe_id: RecipeId) -> Recipe:
        return Recipe(id=recipe_id, title=self.title, servings=self.servings)


class RecipeResponse(ApiModel):
    id: UUID
    title: str
    servings: int

    @classmethod
    def from_domain(cls, recipe: Recipe) -> Self:
        return cls(id=recipe.id, title=recipe.title, servings=recipe.servings)
```

`servings` is checked twice on purpose: the schema gives the client a 422 that names the
field, and the entity keeps the rule for every other way in.
